  "narrative": "Synthesis of the current evidence supports four dataset-level associations with dependency_composite. PTEN loss is now accepted as associated with stronger measured dependency: the discovery signed estimate was +0.239 composite units (95% CI +0.145 to +0.332) for the pre-registered direction of greater dependency, and independent validation gave a larger signed estimate of +0.294 (independent lower bound approximately +0.149, upper +0.439). The independent lower bound is essentially at the 0.15-unit threshold, but the discovery point and interval plus the independent point estimate jointly make a meaningful positive effect more plausible than a null or sub-threshold effect; I accept it as a robust association in this supplied dataset, not as causal evidence or a claim of broad generalizability. MSI-high is accepted as associated with markedly stronger dependency: signed +0.775 (95% CI +0.664 to +0.885). TP53 loss is accepted as associated with weaker measured dependency/higher composite scores: signed +0.829 under the +1-direction claim (95% CI +0.767 to +0.891). The reversed KEAP1-loss claim is also accepted as a dataset-level association: KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker measured dependency by +0.810 (displayed interval +0.745 to +0.875). This reversal is post hoc relative to the original KEAP1 hypothesis, but the effect is large, precise, and internally coherent; it is accepted as a supplied-dataset association and should be treated cautiously until further robustness or independent confirmation. Negative findings are important: lineage subtype, KRAS mutation, BRAF V600E, MTAP loss, STK11 loss, MYC amplification, research marker e positivity, and the original stronger-dependency hypothesis for EGFR amplification did not support the pre-registered effects. EGFR showed a slight opposite-signed effect but not a robust threshold-crossing opposite effect, so it remains a negative finding for the registered claim. The original KEAP1 stronger-dependency hypothesis is rejected because the observed effect was confidently opposite. Remaining uncertainty concerns whether the large accepted signals are mutually confounded: TP53 loss, MSI-high, KEAP1 loss, and PTEN loss may co-occur or correlate with measured technical or biological features. The most informative next analyses are the pre-registered mutual-stratification checks (TP53 effects in MSI-negative cells, MSI effects in TP53-loss-negative cells, lineage-specific effects, Avana-library restriction) plus the new PTEN and KEAP1 stratified checks, including whether PTEN loss remains associated with stronger dependency when TP53 loss and PIK3CA mutation are excluded from the eligible strata, and whether KEAP1-loss weaker dependency persists in TP53-loss-negative cells. These will clarify whether the effects are broad marker associations or are concentrated in correlated genomic backgrounds.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted supplied-dataset associations: (1) MSI_high associated with stronger dependency, direction -1 claim accepted, +0.775. (2) TP53_loss associated with weaker measured dependency/higher composite, direction +1 accepted, +0.829. (3) PTEN_loss associated with stronger dependency, direction -1 accepted after discovery +0.239 and validation +0.294; validation lower bound essentially at 0.15 threshold. (4) KEAP1_loss associated with weaker measured dependency/higher composite, reversal claim accepted, +0.810. Negative/rejected pre-registered contrasts: lineage subtype, KRAS, EGFR in claimed stronger-dependency direction, BRAF, MTAP, STK11, MYC, marker_e. Unresolved/active follow-ups: TP53 and MSI mutual stratification, lineage/library stratification, PTEN in TP53 wild-type and PIK3CA wild-type, KEAP1 in TP53 wild-type, marker_d/f. Interpretation should remain dataset-level: dependency_composite is composite, and effects are associations, not causal knockouts or validated biology. Priority next validation likely H13 KEAP1 reversal if a validation slot is available; alternatively validate the strongest accepted associations if stratification suggests confounding."
}

