  "narrative": "I continue using the signed convention: for direction -1 claims, a positive estimate means stronger measured dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, a positive estimate means a higher/less-negative composite score, i.e. weaker measured dependency. The current accepted supplied-dataset axes are MSI_high and SMARCA4 loss as strong stronger-dependency associations, TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss as strong weaker/higher-composite associations, and PTEN loss as a moderate stronger-dependency association. The most useful next comparisons are not repeats of these main effects, but conditional independence, residual-marker discovery, and context robustness. I retain H48, H58, and H59 as active clean-stratum checks, and I set H53 active because the SMARCA4-loss-exposed cell inside MSI-high cells is expected near the 20-observation minimum but still potentially valid, giving a direct check of whether SMARCA4 loss remains detectable in an MSI-high background.\n\nFor residual discovery in accepted-marker-negative, SMARCA4-intact cells, I add PIK3CA_mutation and SMAD4_loss. PIK3CA is tested in the higher-composite/weaker-dependency direction because an activating pathway mutation could plausibly reduce dependence on some dependency axes, but the expectation is genuinely uncertain. SMAD4 loss is tested in the stronger-dependency direction because loss of a tumor-suppressor signaling component may increase dependency, although it also may be neutral or opposite. These tests ask whether additional threshold-level markers remain after excluding TP53, KEAP1, MSI_high, and SMARCA4 effects.\n\nI add direct conditional mean differences for the two accepted higher-composite axes: KEAP1 loss within TP53-loss cells and TP53 loss within KEAP1-loss cells, both excluding MSI_high and SMARCA4-loss backgrounds. These are refinements of the rejected interaction H31. The H31 interaction was not significant, but direct subgroup estimates are more interpretable for effect size: if each remains threshold-level in the other marker's background, the two accepted effects are mutually independent in this dataset; if one attenuates, it becomes context-dependent rather than invalidating the main accepted association.\n\nI add robustness checks for SMARCA4 loss: a non-Avana library contrast and a squamous-subtype contrast. These are especially important because the dominant library and dominant lineage may carry much of the broad H43 accepted association. If SMARCA4 loss remains strong in non-Avana and squamous cells, the broad association is less likely to be an artifact of the majority library or majority adenocarcinoma cells. If effects are smaller or subthreshold, SMARCA4 should be described as context-sensitive within this dataset, not generalized across technical or histologic strata. H53 complements this by testing SMARCA4 within MSI-high cells.\n\nFinally, I add PTEN_loss conditional tests in TP53-loss cells and in SMARCA4-loss cells with other strong accepted markers absent. PTEN is already independently validated overall and robust in clean, Avana, and KEAP1-intact contexts, but its behavior inside strong accepted-marker backgrounds is less certain. Because PTEN is a moderate effect rather than a near-one-unit effect, the goal is to determine whether it remains above 0.15 units when embedded in another strong dependency axis, or whether it is masked, diluted, or opposite due to co-occurring pathway biology. All proposals are pre-registered falsifiable association tests in the supplied dataset, not causal or mechanistic claims."
