CI +0.121 to +0.333), while H82 tests PTEN loss in squamous cells and gives +0.268 (CI +0.075 to +0.461). Both point estimates are in the expected stronger-dependency direction and are above the 0.15-unit threshold, but both intervals include values below threshold. The broad PTEN main effect remains accepted and independently validated, so these results are supportive rather than decisive. I keep the histologic subgroup claims unresolved and deferred because they should not be treated as confirmed threshold-level context effects on the basis of these intervals alone.\n\nThe CDKN2A results do not establish a robust scientifically meaningful predictor. H72 broad CDKN2A loss is below the threshold: +0.139 (CI +0.065 to +0.213). H73 within TP53-intact cells is also below threshold: +0.125 (CI +0.051 to +0.199). H74 within TP53-loss cells is above threshold in point estimate, +0.188, but highly imprecise and crosses the threshold boundary (CI +0.045 to +0.332). H75 in non-Avana library cells is small and imprecise, +0.068 (CI -0.140 to +0.275). Taken together with earlier clean-stratum CDKN2A estimates, the pattern is borderline, context-sensitive, and not sufficiently precise to accept. I leave these unresolved but deferred rather than accepting CDKN2A as a residual predictor.\n\nGiven the strength and breadth of the TP53-loss evidence, including the new context-robust test H76, I request independent validation of H11, the overall TP53-loss main effect. This is the highest-value unvalidated central association. If validation confirms, the main TP53 weaker-dependency association can be closed; the next main validation target should then be H10 MSI-high. Multiplicity remains relevant, but the accepted new effects are far above the effect-size threshold and directionally consistent with earlier evidence, whereas near-threshold CDKN2A and histologic PTEN subgroup claims remain unresolved. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations, not causal effects or claims of external generalizability.",
