  "narrative": "I use the same signed convention: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger measured dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means a higher raw composite and therefore weaker measured dependency. H16, TP53 loss within adenocarcinoma, gives +0.828 with a lower bound +0.753, far above the 0.15-unit threshold. Together with the accepted squamous TP53-loss test H17, this establishes strong histologic robustness of the TP53-loss weaker-dependency association, so I accept and close H16.\n\nH44 is the formal SMARCA4-loss by lineage interaction, registered in the adenocarcinoma stronger-dependency direction. The signed estimate is negative: -0.174 with 95% interval -0.312 to -0.035. The claimed direction is therefore not supported; the interval excludes positive signed effects. The reverse raw interaction has point magnitude above threshold, but after reversing sign the interval still includes values below 0.15 and does not exclude a near-null modification. The squamous SMARCA4-loss subgroup effect H66 is already very strong, and the formal interaction does not establish a robust new threshold-level histology-specific claim. I reject and close H44 as registered, noting only a possible modest reverse context effect.\n\nH91 is the clean five-marker CDKN2A test excluding TP53 loss, KEAP1 loss, MSI-high, SMARCA4 loss, and PTEN loss. It gives +0.167, interval +0.110 to +0.224. This is the largest CDKN2A clean-stratum estimate and clearly excludes zero, but its lower bound is below the 0.15-unit threshold and its interval overlaps both subthreshold and threshold-level effects. Given the broader pattern of CDKN2A estimates near 0.13-0.19, the evidence is borderline rather than decisive. I do not accept CDKN2A as a scientifically meaningful residual predictor, and I close this investigation as unresolved rather than spend validation on a borderline conditional effect that is unlikely to be resolved by the stricter independent interval.\n\nThe broad non-marker predictors are not supported. H92 Avana library is +0.053 with upper bound +0.141, below threshold for the claimed positive direction. H93 matched RNA/DNA coverage is -0.031 with upper bound +0.045, far below threshold. H94 male sex is -0.083 with interval -0.144 to -0.022 for the claimed positive direction; the opposite signed effect is also below the 0.15 threshold. These technical and demographic comparisons do not reveal a broad non-marker explanation for the accepted dependency pattern, so I reject and close them.\n\nThe constructed research-signature context tests support persistence of the central marker associations in high-signature strata. H95 SMARCA4 loss within high research_signature_a is +0.814, H96 TP53 loss within high research_signature_a is +0.798, H97 KEAP1 loss within high research_signature_a is +0.750, and H98 SMARCA4 loss within high research_signature_b is +0.837; all lower bounds are far above threshold. I accept and close these as context-robustness checks for the large central dataset associations, not as biological mechanism claims.\n\nFor clean-stratum PTEN histology, H99 shows a threshold-level stronger-dependency effect in adenocarcinoma: +0.279 with lower bound +0.186. I accept and close it. H100, the squamous analogue, has point estimate +0.208, but the lower bound is +0.060 and the exposed squamous cell size is small, so the interval overlaps subthreshold effects. Because the main PTEN-loss association is already accepted and independently validated, this squamous conditional test is not worth further validation now; I keep H100 unresolved and deferred. No new validation is requested because the accepted central axes are already validated, the new accepted subgroup results are robustness checks, and the remaining borderline results (CDKN2A and clean squamous PTEN) are not sufficiently precise to make a useful validation target. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
