  "narrative": "I use the same signed convention: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger measured dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means a higher raw composite and therefore weaker measured dependency. The threshold for a scientifically meaningful dataset association is 0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction.\n\nH101-H103 test whether the large SMARCA4-loss, TP53-loss, and KEAP1-loss associations persist in high research_signature_c contexts. H101 is +0.732 with 95% interval +0.583 to +0.881; H102 is +0.820 with interval +0.660 to +0.981; H103 is +0.778 with interval +0.612 to +0.944. All lower bounds are far above 0.15 in the registered directions. I accept and close these as context-robustness evidence for the central marker axes. Because research_signature_c is a constructed standardized assay with no assigned biological role, these results do not establish biological mechanism or generalizability; they mainly show that the large dataset associations are not restricted to previously tested signature strata.\n\nH105 tests MSI-high in a moderate-high research_signature_c context to keep adequate cell counts. It gives +0.785 with interval +0.581 to +0.990, again far above threshold. I accept and close H105. This further supports context robustness of the MSI-high stronger-dependency axis.\n\nH104 tests PTEN loss in high research_signature_c. The signed estimate is only +0.069 with interval -0.216 to +0.354, and the exposed cell has 40 observations. The point estimate is far below the 0.15 threshold, the interval includes both zero and threshold-level values, and the direction is not supported. Although the overall PTEN-loss association is already accepted and validated, this particular context does not provide evidence for a threshold-level PTEN-specific association. I reject and close H104 as a context test, not as a rejection of the main PTEN-loss association.\n\nH111 and H112 test SMARCA4 loss and TP53 loss among samples positive for both constructed research markers d and e. H111 is +0.803 with interval +0.666 to +0.939; H112 is +0.791 with interval +0.665 to +0.917. Both are very strong and robust in the registered directions. I accept and close them as further context-robustness checks for the largest central associations. Again, the constructed marker context has no assigned biological interpretation.\n\nThe broad non-marker comparisons are null or subthreshold. H106 media serum 10 versus 5 gives -0.043 with interval -0.114 to +0.028 for a claimed positive direction; the interval upper bound is below threshold. H107 screen batch_a versus batch_b gives -0.030 with interval -0.113 to +0.052, far below threshold. H108 humagne_cd library status gives -0.057 with interval -0.185 to +0.071; the claimed positive direction is not supported, and the possible reverse effect is imprecise and not decisive. H109 media serum 10 versus 2 gives +0.003 with interval -0.076 to +0.083. H110 has_rna_omics gives -0.037 with interval -0.122 to +0.049. None reaches the threshold in the registered direction. I reject and close H106-H110. These results strengthen the earlier conclusion that the accepted marker-axis association map is not explained by these measured culture-condition, screening-batch, library, RNA-availability, or demographic covariates.\n\nNo new independent validation is requested. The newly accepted results are context-robustness checks for central associations that are already validated at the main-effect level, and the newly rejected results are either clearly subthreshold or too imprecise/borderline to make useful validation targets. The remaining borderline PTEN context contrasts and CDKN2A results are not sufficiently robust to justify spending validation now."
