  "narrative": "Interpretation follows the signed convention: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed or subgroup condition; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed or subgroup condition. The meaningful-effect threshold is 0.15 composite dependency-score units.\n\nThe simple subgroup robustness comparisons are strong. In lower-screen-quality subsets (screen_roc_auc <= 0.93), SMARCA4 loss shows +0.768 [0.642, 0.895], TP53 loss +0.840 [0.708, 0.971], KEAP1 loss +0.854 [0.719, 0.989], and MSI-high +0.839 [0.559, 1.118]. In slower-growth subsets (growth_rate_doublings_per_day <= 0.348), SMARCA4 loss shows +0.875 [0.745, 1.004], TP53 loss +0.846 [0.709, 0.983], KEAP1 loss +0.844 [0.706, 0.982], and MSI-high +0.498 [0.220, 0.776]. All point estimates and lower bounds exceed the threshold and exclude zero, so I accept H208-H215 as dataset-level evidence that the main marker associations persist in these technical and proliferation contexts. The MSI checks use exposed cells of 28 and 25, respectively, so they meet the minimum-cell rule but are less precise; their lower bounds are still above threshold. These subgroup results should not be read as effect-modification results because they are not interaction contrasts.\n\nThe direct PTEN-loss interaction contrasts are much less supportive of effect modification. H206 high hypoxia gives +0.102 [-0.102, 0.306], which is below threshold and includes the null. H207 high Wnt gives +0.184 [-0.011, 0.379], H216 lower screen quality gives +0.173 [-0.031, 0.376], and H217 faster growth gives +0.171 [-0.053, 0.396]. The latter three point estimates exceed 0.15, but their intervals include negative values and therefore include no interaction; the uncertainty is too wide to accept a meaningful PTEN interaction. I record these as unresolved/closed rather than accepted, because the evidence does not support modification, while the intervals are not sufficiently narrow to justify a definitive rejection of a modest positive modification. Overall, the data do not establish PTEN effect modification by hypoxia, Wnt activity, screen quality, or growth rate.\n\nMultiplicity remains a concern: many context-specific comparisons have been run, and some intervals include meaningful positive effects merely because of imprecision. Independent validation capacity is exhausted, so these are discovery associations in the supplied dataset only. They do not establish causality, single-gene mechanism, pathway meaning, or generalizability beyond this dataset.",
