  "narrative": "No response deadlines are listed and no assessment requests are due, and the controller reports no remaining new independent validation requests. I therefore assessed all validation estimates currently present in the ledger as evidence already reflected in the maintained claim set: H4/R79 supports rejection of a meaningful BRAF V600E weaker-dependency association; H7/H13/R52 supports robust KEAP1-loss weaker-dependency; H8/R13 supports PTEN-loss stronger-dependency; H10/R92 supports MSI-high stronger-dependency; H11/R78 supports TP53-loss weaker-dependency; H28/R39 supports PTEN loss in KEAP1-negative cells; H40/R38 and H43/R65 support SMARCA4-loss stronger-dependency; H57/R105 does not support SMARCA4-loss modification by high signature A; H116/R130 supports the main PTEN-loss association in high-MYC cells but is imprecise; H127/R143, H158/R156, and H165/R169 are independent evidence against robust CDKN2A-loss association in high-MYC and aneuploid contexts. The accepted controller-maintained set is unchanged.\n\nMain supported findings in the supplied 2000-line dataset. The outcome threshold is 0.15 composite dependency-score units. More negative dependency_composite means stronger dependency. Under the signed convention, direction -1 claims are positive when the exposed condition shows stronger dependency, and direction +1 claims are positive when the exposed condition shows weaker dependency. SMARCA4 loss is a strong stronger-dependency association: discovery H43/R40 is +0.804 [0.742, 0.867] and validation R65 is +0.803 [0.712, 0.894]. MSI-high is a strong stronger-dependency association: H10/R10 is +0.775 [0.664, 0.885] and validation R92 is +0.831 [0.668, 0.993]. TP53 loss is a strong weaker-dependency association: H11/R11 is +0.829 [0.767, 0.891] and validation R78 is +0.771 [0.673, 0.868]. KEAP1 loss is a strong weaker-dependency association: H13/R7 is +0.810 [0.745, 0.875] and validation R52 is +0.840 [0.754, 0.926]. PTEN loss is a moderate stronger-dependency association: H8/R8 is +0.239 [0.145, 0.332] and validation R13 is +0.294 [0.149, 0.439]. These are dataset-level associations, not causal or mechanistic claims, and the endpoint is composite rather than a single-gene knockout measurement.\n\nContext-specific subgroup means remain robustness evidence, not established effect modification. The four strongest marker associations and the PTEN association appear visible in many clinical, technical, omics, lineage, screen-quality, growth-rate, mutation-burden, and pathway-score contexts. However, direct interaction tests generally do not establish that the effect changes across those contexts. SMARCA4-loss modifications by subtype, signature A, lower screen quality, and aneuploidy are not supported; MSI-high subtype interaction is rejected; KEAP1 interaction with SMARCA4 loss is rejected. PTEN interactions with lineage, PIK3CA status, KEAP1-negative status, AXL all-population, lower screen quality, fast growth, hypoxia, stemness, and core-negative hypoxia, stemness, or screen quality do not provide accepted evidence of modification. The most notable direct interaction signals remain imprecise rather than conclusive: all-population PTEN high-Wnt interaction R207 is +0.184 [-0.011, 0.379]; all-population PTEN lower-screen-quality interaction R216 is +0.173 [-0.031, 0.376]; all-population PTEN fast-growth interaction R217 is +0.171 [-0.053, 0.396]. These are compatible with possible PTEN modification but do not support acceptance under the stated interaction standard.\n\nTwo reversed attenuation signals should be interpreted cautiously. H229 registered a stronger PTEN effect in core-negative high-MYC cells versus complement and was rejected for that direction; its signed estimate is -0.187 [-0.343, -0.031], which favors attenuation of the PTEN association in high-MYC core-negative cells. The reversed interval is +0.187 [0.031, 0.343], with point above threshold but lower bound below threshold, so it is suggestive but not accepted. Similarly, H225 rejected a stronger MSI-high effect in slower-growing cells, while favoring attenuation; the reversed interval is +0.368 [0.064, 0.672], again with lower bound below threshold. These are possible attenuation patterns, not established effect modification.\n\nNegative findings are broad. Many predictors show no meaningful association at the 0.15 threshold: lineage subtype, KRAS mutation, EGFR amplification, BRAF V600E, MTAP loss, STK11 loss, MYC amplification, research marker D, E, and F, sex, CRISPR-library indicators, omics availability, screen batch, media serum concentration contrasts, and several secondary core-negative markers. In particular, the constructed research markers and signatures do not behave as main dependency predictors, and their high-subgroup marker associations mostly reflect the robust main marker effects rather than evidence of constructed-assay modification.\n\nCDKN2A loss is not robustly supported. Several core-negative discovery contrasts are near or above threshold, including R30, R48, and R95, but independent validation failed in high-MYC cells R143, high-aneuploidy cells R156, and core-negative high-aneuploidy cells R169. Direct CDKN2A interactions tested so far also do not establish modification. Invalid underpowered contrasts are design limitations, but they are not evidence of association. Therefore the best current conclusion is that CDKN2A loss is not a robustly associated dependency predictor in this dataset after validation and multiplicity.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. The main unresolved scientific issue is PTEN-loss effect modification. Existing robust subgroup means show PTEN associations in many contexts, but direct interactions are mostly null or imprecise. The high-Wnt, high-AXL, fast-growth, lower-screen-quality, high-MYC, and slower-growth interactions remain useful follow-up targets. The MSI-high slower-growth attenuation signal is a secondary unresolved question. The four registered proposals are focused discovery interactions rather than new validations because validation capacity is exhausted: direct PTEN-loss interaction with high Wnt and high AXL within the core-negative population; a formally reversed PTEN-loss high-MYC attenuation proposal; and a formally reversed MSI-high slower-growth attenuation proposal. For PTEN proposals, the hypothesis is that the PTEN-loss association is stronger in the named context than in the complementary core-negative cells; direction -1 makes a positive signed interaction evidence for that stronger context effect. For reversed attenuation proposals, direction +1 makes a positive signed interaction evidence that the marker association is weaker in the named context. Future acceptance should require the interaction interval to support the claimed direction and be compatible with the 0.15 threshold; otherwise robust subgroup means should be reported as robustness only. Insufficient cell counts, if encountered, should be treated as design limitations rather than empirical nulls.\n\nFinal scientific status. The supplied dataset supports SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high as strong stronger-dependency markers, TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss as strong weaker-dependency markers, and PTEN loss as a moderate stronger-dependency marker. It does not support broad technical, demographic, or most secondary molecular predictors. It does not establish meaningful effect modification of the major marker effects by the contexts tested so far, although PTEN contexts and two attenuation patterns remain possible but unresolved. CDKN2A is not robust. All conclusions are dataset-specific associations and do not establish causality, single-gene knockout mechanism, biological pathway interpretation, or generalizability beyond this dataset.",
